# Context Cancellation with Worker Pools

## Pattern
Use a bounded worker pool with `context.Context` to control concurrency and enable graceful cancellation. Each worker checks for cancellation before processing.

## CORRECT
```go
func ProcessBatch(ctx context.Context, items []string) error {
    itemsCh := make(chan string, len(items))
    for _, item := range items {
        itemsCh <- item
    }
    close(itemsCh)

    errCh := make(chan error, 1)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    numWorkers := 5

    for i := 0; i < numWorkers; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            for item := range itemsCh {
                select {
                case <-ctx.Done():
                    return // worker stops, but items in channel are lost
                default:
                    if err := processItem(ctx, item); err != nil {
                        errCh <- err
                        return
                    }
                }
            }
        }()
    }

    wg.Wait()
    close(errCh)

    // Return first error if any, or nil
    for err := range errCh {
        if err != nil {
            return err
        }
    }
    return nil
}

func main() {
    ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 30*time.Second)
    defer cancel()

    items := []string{"a", "b", "c"}
    if err := ProcessBatch(ctx, items); err != nil {
        log.Printf("batch failed: %v", err)
    }
}
```

## WRONG - No cancellation check
```go
func ProcessBatch(ctx context.Context, items []string) error {
    // Workers never check ctx.Done() – will continue even after cancellation
    // ...
}
```

## Rules
1. Context is first parameter: `func Do(ctx context.Context, ...)`
2. Always call `cancel()` via `defer` to prevent leaks
3. Use a channel to distribute work – close it to signal no more items
4. Check `ctx.Done()` in the main select inside each worker
5. Limit concurrency with a fixed number of goroutines (worker pool)

## Common Patterns
```go
// Worker pool with context
func worker(ctx context.Context, jobs <-chan Job, results chan<- Result) {
    for job := range jobs {
        select {
        case <-ctx.Done():
            return
        default:
            results <- process(ctx, job)
        }
    }
}
```

